### Paragraph-based chunking (built 2026-10-27, branch `paragraph-chunking`; `fixed` stays the default)

Today `chunker.py` uses `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter` (1000 chars, 150 overlap): it prefers `

` breaks but cuts wherever the size limit falls, so a paragraph can be split mid-thought or packed with the end of the previous one. Chunks never cross a page (PDF) or section (DOCX/TXT).

- `CHUNK_STRATEGY=paragraph|fixed` (default `fixed`). Paragraph mode (`chunker.paragraph_spans` + `pack_paragraphs`): one chunk per paragraph, shorter than `MIN_CHUNK_CHARS` (200) merged with the next (the last with the previous), longer than `MAX_CHUNK_CHARS` (1200) split between sentences (between words for an overlong sentence). No overlap. A chunk is a verbatim slice of the cleaned text, so offsets and quote verification are unchanged
- Cap chunk size to the embedder: `CHUNK_SIZE` ≤ 2000 and `MAX_CHUNK_CHARS + MIN_CHUNK_CHARS` ≤ 2000 (`config.EMBED_MAX_CHARS`), checked at startup
- Paragraph detection per format: PDF `wrap` (blank line; a line ending `.!?` shorter than 85% of the page's 80th-percentile line; a bullet / `1.` / `(a)` on the next line; not `:`, which split "Plan 1:
$20.00" tables), DOCX/HTML `paragraph` (every line, table rows kept together), TXT `auto` (wrapped if the 90th-percentile line is ≤ 120 chars, else one paragraph per line, e.g. `bsb.txt` verses)
- Paragraphs that run over a PDF page break (2026-10-27, branch `cross-page-paragraphs`): in paragraph mode, pages joined by a running-on paragraph are chunked as one text (`chunker.continues_on_next_page`, `_split_page_paragraphs`), so a chunk crosses a page only where a paragraph does. The test at the break is the in-page `wrap` rule, but stricter, since cutting at the page is the safe default: the next page must start lowercase, with a digit or with a currency sign (not a heading, not "®"), and a page with under 2 lines never continues. New `chunks.page_end` column (via `ADDED_COLUMNS`) + Chroma metadata, carried through `RetrievedChunk`, the prompt ("pp. 4-5"), trace rows, agent step summaries, claims and `GET /documents/{id}/chunks/{idx}`. Source panel renders every page of the chunk (`claimPages`) and finds the quote across them; old answers without `page_end` show one page as before. Fixed mode never crosses a page
  - Found: `services_fees.pdf` alternates running heads ("Business accounts and services 5" on odd pages, "4 RBC Royal Bank" on even), each on under half the pages and with a changing number, so `clean_pages` kept them; every page break looked like a continuation and glued them in (20 of 108 chunks). Fixed for **both** strategies: a leading / trailing number within `MAX_PAGE_OFFSET` (10) of the page's own counts as a page number, and lines are also counted on odd and even pages separately (groups of ≥ 3 pages). Fees: 824 chars of running heads dropped; 0 chunks cross a page (it is laid out page by page), which is right
  - Unit tests: suite 268/268 (continuation rule, one- and three-page runs, offsets across the break, fixed never sets `page_end`, alternating heads, a far number isn't a page number, pipeline stores `page_end` in SQLite + Chroma, prompt "pp. 6-7", chunk API); frontend 34/34 (`claimPages`, `sourceLine` range, step pages)
  - Existing documents keep their old chunks (the `chunking` label didn't change): `rebuild-index --rechunk-all` to pick up the header fix
- Heading as context: already the case for both strategies (`pipeline._embed_text` and the BM25 rows prefix the section heading; stored text is unchanged)
- Optional later: semantic chunking (split where the embedding similarity between neighbouring sentences drops)
- Re-index: each document stores `chunking` (`Settings.chunking`, e.g. `paragraph:200-1200`) and `indexed_at` (new columns via `ADDED_COLUMNS`). `python -m app.ingestion.cli rebuild-index --rechunk` re-parses, re-chunks and re-embeds the documents chunked differently (`--rechunk-all`: every one), then rebuilds the index once (`pipeline.rechunk_documents`). BM25's cache key now includes `indexed_at`, so the API notices a re-chunk done from the CLI even when the chunk count is unchanged. `chunks` preview prints the chunking
